						<a href="/forum/t-13947625/jejunu:the-curious-embodiment-of-a-manmade-famine-looking-fo">Jejunu: The Curious Embodiment of a Manmade Famine (Looking for Greenlight)</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4852561">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>What like&#8230;actually is 'Jejunu'? Is it a person? A creature? An object? Some weird hostile meme that doesn't exist as a physical thing but as the noosphere embodiment of the concept of 'famine'? How does Jejunu tie into the 'manmade famine' thing?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A few interviews and tests lead to the discovery of it’s abilities and it’s constant need to eat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So like&#8230;what are it's abilities? How do the interviews and tests lead to this discovery? What makes this discovery interesting for a reader to read about?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Jejunu makes an escape, learns a few things about the facility, and is subsequently placed back into confinement with ease.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How did it manage to escape if it was so easily reconfined?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>With each interview it becomes more uncomfortable and hungry until an incident inevitably happens.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What incident?</p>
<hr />
<p>I guess to summarise my feelings on this, I would say that I don't really have a clear idea of what you're doing to offer much in depth feedback. My largest concern isn't so much 'I don't really have an idea of what this is' so much as that I am concerned that all of the narrative is focussed around info-dumping about the SCP, when really I want to see some kinda&#8230;exploration of why this thing existing is interesting.</p>
<p>It also feels like it's strongly lacking a hook: there's this kinda, progression of events that kinda works but there's not really a driving force moving from one to the next or a sense of the events building up to something or something, y'know?</p>
</div>
