<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I think at the core of this, my issue with your pitch is that I don't find any of the individual elements interesting and I also don't find the way the elements come together particularly interesting.</p>
<p>The creation/destruction of whole timelines is something that's really hard to pull off in an article well because like&#8230;it's really hard to relate to big numbers changing? Like, this really needs a human face and like, someone who we can relate to that this affects: see e.g. <a href="/scp-5552">SCP-5552</a> where like, yeah, the narrative involves massive death and huge changes and stakes; but like, it works because it's focussed in on a few researchers trying to make things work, and academic theft.</p>
<p>Following this: the central narrative section doesn't actually do anything interesting with the huge world ending stakes: there's this vague thing about the Foundation stopping it being used; and them needing the blue button as a last resort; but there's not really any indications of like&#8230;.stuff happening to show why it's a bad idea to use this or how the blue button could be used as a last resort or anything.</p>
<p>You mention having 'an incident report, an interview log, testing logs, and addenda' but I have literally 0 idea of what you're actually doing with them.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

